  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nShortwaves rotate through the broader trough today and Thursday\nbringing chances for showers and thunderstorms, some of which \nmay be capable of producing strong gusty winds. With continued \nnorthwesterly flow, moisture will be on the lower end with PWATs\njust over an inch by the afternoon. This also continues our \ncooling trend with high temperatures in the upper 70s and low \n80s south of the mountains, and in the low to mid-70s to the \nnorth. Mostly clear skies may allow for heating to develop a \ncouple hundred joules of instability, and with 25-30 kts of \nshear storm development is not out of the question. The limiting\nfactor is going to be the lack of moisture which should keep \ncoverage isolated. Still, with dewpoint depressions around 20C a\ngusty storm is not out of the question.\n\nThursday looks like the better chance for strong storms as the \nforcing for ascent is stronger with the trough axis swinging \nthrough. Moisture will still be on the lower end so coverage may\nend up similar if not slightly more than today, but the \ninstability parameters look higher. Again, mostly clear skies \nshould allow CAPE of 500-1500 J/kg to develop with around 30 kts\nof shear. Halfway decent lapse rates in the mid (6.5-7 C/km) \nand low levels (7.5-8 C/ km) bring a gusty wind threat again \nwith any stronger storms that develop, but both the CAMs and \nCSUs machine learning model have been noticeably backing off. \nThe HRRR has been liking a round of early morning showers that \nmakes it harder for later stuff to develop, where as the NAM \nNest holds off until the afternoon with a broken line of showers\nand storms crossing through the evening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nShowers potentially linger through the better part of Friday as\nthe 500 mb trough remains overhead, but models are in good \nagreement that high pressure begins building into the area by \nthe evening so most areas should see improvement. Friday could \nsee some gusty winds as the pressure gradient tightens with a \nbreeze carrying into Saturday as well. It is a sharp trough axis\nthat moves off to the east so increased northwesterly flow is \ngoing to bring us some cool nights with lows in the 40s and low \n50s.\n\nSunday looks to remain mostly dry, but high pressure will be \nmaking its exit and the 500 mb flow transitions back to zonal. \nThe pattern becomes unsettled as global models bring low \npressure into the region early next week, but unsurprisingly at \nthis time range the track and timing is uncertain. For now the \nNBM having blanket 30-50% PoPs late Sunday night through Monday \nseems reasonable",
